@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+---
+tier: quick
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: in-progress
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/app.py, src/flask/ctx.py, tests/test_appctx.py, tests/test_reqctx.py, tests/test_signals.py]
+---
+
+# d17-teardown-resilience
+
+## Why
+Teardown callbacks or signal receivers that raise currently stop later cleanup,
+and an application-context teardown error can prevent context-local restoration.
+
+## What
+- Collect errors while invoking every request- and application-context teardown
+  callback and receiver, then raise them after cleanup.
+- Restore context-local state before a collected teardown error leaves
+  `AppContext.pop`.
+- Keep Python 3.10 compatibility by raising the first error when exception
+  groups are unavailable.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Every registered teardown callback and signal receiver is attempted after
+  an earlier error. — check: `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests/test_appctx.py -q`
+- [x] Context-local request and application state is cleared before teardown
+  errors propagate. — check: `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests/test_appctx.py -q`
+- [x] Multiple teardown errors are exposed together on Python versions with
+  exception groups. — check: `PYTHONPATH=src:.bench-pydeps python3 -m pytest tests/test_appctx.py -q`
+
+## Impact
+`AppContext.pop` is the common request/application context exit path, including
+test request contexts and normal WSGI requests. The public `do_teardown_*`
+methods are retained, but use the same collection mechanism so direct callers
+also receive cleanup only after all registered callbacks and receivers run.
+CLI and application-only contexts use the application-context branch; no route,
+data, permission, or external API behavior is changed beyond D-17's teardown
+failure contract.
